re, a high degree of competence in civil engineering specialties such as surveying and mapping\n\n到貨時通知我\n\n3.Communication for Professional Engineers\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA singular mathematical promenade, Etienne Ghys - Shop Online for Books in Australia (https://www.fishpond.com.au/Books/singular-mathematical-promenade-Etienne-Ghys/9782847889390)\nciteturn72search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\nA singular mathematical promenade\n\nBy\n\nRating\n\n | \n\nProduct Description\n\nProduct Details\n\n\nImage\nAsk a Question About this Product More...\nImage\n\n[Input]  \n\nLook for similar items by category\n\nHome » Books » French » Mathématiques et sciences » Mathématiques » Algèbre\n\nHome » Books » Science » Mathematics » Topology\n\nHome » Books » Science » Mathematics » Research\n\nThis title is unavailable for purchase as none of our regular suppliers have stock available. If you are the publisher, author or distributor for this item, please visit this link.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA singular mathematical promenade (https://perso.ens-lyon.fr/ghys/reviews/Munoz.pdf)\nciteturn72search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: 5.9 years ago; \nA singular mathematical promenade\n\nThis book is a small jewel for a mathematical library. As the author defines it, it is a promenade into the mathematical world. With the excuse of a simple mathematical question, the author enters into many realms of mathematics, some of them almost recreative, other bits are truly deep mathematics concepts, all of that flavoured with historical review of many developments in topology, geometry, algebra and analysis.\n\nFirst the question proposed by the author: if one has a collection of real polynomials \\(P_1(x), \\ldots, P_k(x)\\), that are ordered in some way for small \\(x > 0\\), and in other way for small \\(x < 0\\), how this ordering may change? This is rewritten as how the branches of real algebraic curves are cyclically ordered at a point. Surprisingly, there is a neat characterization (not any order is allowed), and one may count how many possibilities are, with nice combinatorics popping up. The book explores very different questions related to this problem, and follows on different ramifications. The author has done good work in accounting on the history of some very classical notions (just to name a few, the fundamental theorem of algebra, or the theory of Puiseux series, the linking number of knots, but there are very many), and he has made a very loable effort (and very successful indeed!) to transmitting this in a friendly, amusing and rigorous manner. Notions of combinatorics, discrete mathematics (like the analysis of trees), algebra (operads), algebraic geometry (resolution of curve singularities, complex singularities), are explained in an enlightening way, changing mathematical rigour for clarifying drawings in a wise choice.\n\nThe layout of the book is also impressive: it contains many nice photographs, pictures and drawings, also scans of manuscripts, historial bibliographic references, references of papers and books on mathematical concepts, clarifying remarks, all distributed in a column at the right of the page (so that one has the information immediately available). The text contains many historical quotations in different languages, with translations, and interesting analysis of the mathematics of our \"classics\" (Newton, Gauss, Hipparchus, are among hundreds of other names mentioned along the text).\n\nOne last word. The book is available for free at the arxiv: https://arxiv.org/ftp/arxiv/papers/1612/1612.06373.pdf  \nIt is published with a price of 27 euros. Just let me say that, after reading the copy of my library for writing this review, I will buy a personal copy for myself. I really enjoyed it!\n\nReviewer: Vicente Munoz  \nAffiliation: Universidad Complutense de Madrid\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBook details panel with the same descriptive blurb, and labeled fields: Author: Étienne Ghys; Publisher: ENS Editions; Published: 2017; ISBN: 978-2-84788-939-0; Price: 27 euros; Pages: 302; link: http://www.ens-lyon.fr/editions/catalogue\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nCategorisation panel listing three categories: Algebraic And Complex Geometry; History Of Mathematics; Mathematics Education And Popularization Of Mathematics\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nFooter line with user/date metadata: Submitted by Vicente Munoz | 14 / Feb / 2018\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe MAA's Basic Library List (https://www.coe.psu.ac.th/ad/bll/index.html)\nciteturn72search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; \nA Singular Mathematical Promenade  | Étienne Ghys  | ENS Editions  | 2017  | *  | Biography | Singularities | Surveys\nReckoning with Risk: Learning to Live With Uncertainty  | Gerd Gigerenzer  | Gardners Books  | 2003  |    | ad\nGeometry and its Applications in Arts, Nature and Technology  | Georg Glaeser  | Springer  | 2020  |    | Geometry\nChaos: Making a New Science  | James Gleick  | Penguin Books  | 1988  | ***  | Surveys\nIncompleteness: The Proof and Paradox of Kurt Gödel  | Rebecca Goldstein  | W.W. Norton  | 2006  |    | ad\nPolyominoes  | Solomon W. Golomb  | Princeton Univ Press  | 1996  | **  | Recreational\nApplied Mathematics: A Very Short Introduction  | Alain Goriely  | Oxford Univ Press  | 2018  |    | Applied\nNaming Infinity  | Loren Graham and Jean-Michel Kantor  | Belknap Press  | 2009  |    | Set Theory | Philosophy | Logic | History\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy Guide (https://upcommons.upc.edu/bitstreams/94738d48-1489-4407-9b53-0dd11ee5d3a8/download)\nciteturn72search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; \nEXAMINATION RULES.\nThe problem 